Elmi Guray Academy.

2
00:00:02.472 --> 00:00:04.538
Lesson 5.

3
00:00:04.538 --> 00:00:07.391
Categories of AI Tools.

4
00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:24.844
Lesson Overview Course AI Tools for Developers Lesson Lesson 5 Topic Categories of AI Development Tools Level Beginner Learning Objectives By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: Identify four important categories of AI tools used by developers.

5
00:00:24.844 --> 00:00:30.945
Explain the difference between an external AI chatbot and a project-aware coding tool.

6
00:00:30.945 --> 00:00:34.872
Explain how intelligent code completion works.

7
00:00:34.872 --> 00:00:39.994
Describe what coding agents can do across multiple project files.

8
00:00:39.994 --> 00:00:44.763
Explain how AI code-review tools support human reviewers.

9
00:00:44.763 --> 00:00:49.749
Select an appropriate AI tool for a specific development task.

10
00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:55.537
Central idea: AI development tools do not all perform the same job.

11
00:00:55.537 --> 00:01:01.964
A responsible developer must understand which tool category is suitable for each task.

12
00:01:01.964 --> 00:01:08.567
English Technical Section Developers now have access to several types of AI-powered tools.

13
00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:17.453
Although many of these tools use similar AI technology, they interact with developers and software projects in different ways.

14
00:01:17.453 --> 00:01:27.426
The four important categories covered in this lesson are: 1 External AI Chatbots External AI chatbots are general conversation tools.

15
00:01:27.426 --> 00:01:38.635
Developers can use them to ask questions, learn programming concepts, plan projects, analyze short code examples, and discuss possible solutions.

16
00:01:38.635 --> 00:01:46.257
Examples mentioned in this course include: ChatGPT Gemini Good uses Learning a programming concept.

17
00:01:46.257 --> 00:01:48.662
Planning a new project.

18
00:01:48.662 --> 00:01:51.366
Explaining an error message.

19
00:01:51.366 --> 00:01:54.586
Discussing different solution ideas.

20
00:01:54.586 --> 00:01:57.344
Generating a small example.

21
00:01:57.344 --> 00:02:04.844
Main limitation A normal external chatbot does not automatically know everything inside your local project.

22
00:02:04.844 --> 00:02:14.437
It can only work with project information that you provide through your message, uploaded files, connected services, or another supported method.

23
00:02:14.437 --> 00:02:21.380
2 AI Code-Completion Tools Code-completion tools work inside or alongside a code editor.

24
00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:30.157
They examine the current file, nearby code, and other available context to predict what the developer may want to type next.

25
00:02:30.157 --> 00:02:37.263
Examples covered in this course include: GitHub Copilot Cursor What makes the completion intelligent?

26
00:02:37.263 --> 00:02:43.676
Traditional autocomplete may suggest a variable, method, or property from a known list.

27
00:02:43.676 --> 00:02:49.029
AI-powered completion can often suggest: A complete line of code.

28
00:02:49.029 --> 00:02:51.719
A group of related lines.

29
00:02:51.719 --> 00:02:54.450
An HTML structure.

30
00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:57.820
A function based on its name or comment.

31
00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:01.026
Code that matches the surrounding pattern.

32
00:03:01.026 --> 00:03:08.499
The developer remains in control and decides whether to accept, modify, or reject each suggestion.

33
00:03:08.499 --> 00:03:16.054
3 AI Coding Agents A coding agent can perform work that is larger than a normal autocomplete suggestion.

34
00:03:16.054 --> 00:03:31.298
Depending on its permissions and instructions, an agent may inspect the project, create files, update existing files, search for related code, run commands, and attempt to complete a multi-step development task.

35
00:03:31.298 --> 00:03:40.361
Examples mentioned in the lesson include: GitHub Copilot Cursor Claude Code Possible agent tasks Create a complete webpage.

36
00:03:40.361 --> 00:03:43.459
Update several related files.

37
00:03:43.459 --> 00:03:46.488
Find where a feature is implemented.

38
00:03:46.488 --> 00:03:49.545
Repair a bug across a project.

39
00:03:49.545 --> 00:03:52.399
Add a new component or route.

40
00:03:52.399 --> 00:03:55.999
Run tests and react to test results.

41
00:03:55.999 --> 00:04:02.358
Important: An agent can make changes quickly, but speed does not guarantee correctness.

42
00:04:02.358 --> 00:04:08.064
The developer must provide clear instructions and review every important change.

43
00:04:08.064 --> 00:04:18.377
4 AI Code-Review Tools AI code-review tools examine code changes and provide feedback before those changes are accepted into the main project.

44
00:04:18.377 --> 00:04:23.554
They are commonly used with pull requests on platforms such as GitHub.

45
00:04:23.554 --> 00:04:29.545
Examples mentioned in this lesson include: BugBot CodeRabbit What might they detect?

46
00:04:29.545 --> 00:04:31.665
Possible bugs.

47
00:04:31.665 --> 00:04:34.111
Missing error handling.

48
00:04:34.111 --> 00:04:36.584
Security concerns.

49
00:04:36.584 --> 00:04:38.676
Repeated code.

50
00:04:38.676 --> 00:04:40.931
Unclear logic.

51
00:04:40.931 --> 00:04:43.717
Possible performance problems.

52
00:04:43.717 --> 00:04:51.747
Their feedback can give a human reviewer a useful starting point, but it should not automatically replace human judgment.

53
00:04:51.747 --> 00:05:03.146
Example: Asking an External Chatbot A developer could ask a general chatbot: Explain the difference between an HTML class and an HTML id.

54
00:05:03.146 --> 00:05:06.339
Give me one simple example of each.

55
00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:12.439
This question does not require the chatbot to understand an entire private project.

56
00:05:12.439 --> 00:05:15.483
It is a general programming question.

57
00:05:15.483 --> 00:05:21.325
Example: Giving a Coding Agent Instructions A developer could instruct a

58
00:05:21.508 --> 00:05:27.055
project-aware coding agent: Inspect the existing registration page.

59
00:05:27.005 --> 00:05:32.617
Add a required preferred-language field with two choices: English and Somali.

60
00:05:32.617 --> 00:05:35.185
Keep the existing design.

61
00:05:35.185 --> 00:05:38.201
Do not change the payment logic.

62
00:05:38.201 --> 00:05:41.027
Show me the files you changed.

63
00:05:41.027 --> 00:05:44.927
This request relates to a specific project.

64
00:05:44.927 --> 00:05:51.530
The agent needs access to the relevant project files and must understand how they work together.

65
00:05:51.530 --> 00:05:57.875
Professional habit: Give coding agents narrow, clear, testable instructions.

66
00:05:57.875 --> 00:06:01.679
Review the result before moving to the next task.

